      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buffalo%2C_Kansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from Buffalo Creek, itself named after the American bison that once roamed the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buffalo%2C_Kansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Buffalo’s growth accelerated in 1886 when the Missouri Pacific Railroad ran through the town, boosting commerce.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buffalo%2C_Kansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The first store opened in 1869, followed by the first hotel in 1870, marking Buffalo’s early commercial beginnings.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buffalo%2C_Kansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Buffalo was officially incorporated as a city in 1898, solidifying its municipal status.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buffalo%2C_Kansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Geographically, Buffalo sits at 37°42′34″N 95°41′49″W, placing it in southeastern Kansas near the border with Oklahoma.",
